# Generated artifacts — the record is specs, reports, provenance JSON and sha256 manifests,
# never the binaries themselves.
outputs/
scratch/
*.mp4
*.mov
*.webm
# `.mkv` is the container this repository's OWN encoder writes by default, and it was the one
# missing from this list. `tools/encode_control.py`'s CODECS table gives `.mkv` to three of
# its five profiles — including the default `--codec ffv1-gbrp`, the lossless one, which is
# the largest file the pipeline produces — takes its destination from an operator-supplied
# `--out` with no directory constraint, and creates whatever directory that names;
# `tools/fetch_run.py` carries `VIDEO_SUFFIXES = ('.mp4', '.webm', '.mkv')`, so the fetcher
# expects the same container back from a provider. Measured with `git check-ignore -v` from a
# worktree on `63191ee`: `run/x.mp4` -> IGNORED, `outputs/x.mkv` -> IGNORED (by the directory
# rule), and `a.mkv` at the repo root -> NOT IGNORED. That is the `sheets/control_0001.png`
# measurement in the block below, one container over. `tests/test_packaging.py` now DERIVES
# the requirement from `encode_control.CODECS` and `fetch_run.VIDEO_SUFFIXES`, so the next
# codec brings its extension with it instead of relying on this list being remembered.
*.mkv
*.glb
*.gltf
*.blend
*.blend1
*.exr
*.npy

# Raster images. This list did not have them until it was measured: every control sequence,
# start frame and comparison sheet this repo produces is a PNG (`armature_core.pngio` is a
# dependency-free PNG writer), and `git check-ignore` reported `sheets/control_0001.png`,
# `renders/frame.png` and `tools/out/plate.jpg` all NOT IGNORED. The stated backstop covered
# every artifact type except the dominant one.
*.png
*.jpg
*.jpeg
*.webp
*.tif
*.tiff

# The committed figures, re-included by name. These are the two directories that actually
# hold committed images — the previous negation pointed at `docs/figures/`, which does not
# exist in this tree, so it re-included nothing. `tests/test_packaging.py` fails on a
# negation whose path is absent, and on a committed figure the rules above swallow.
!docs/assets/**
!site/public/**

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# Build artifacts. The published package is built by CI from a tag, never from a local
# tree, so a wheel that rode a commit could only ever disagree with the one users get.
dist/
build/
# `npm/*.tgz` only covered a pack run from inside npm/; `npm pack npm` from the root drops
# `armature-studio-0.3.0.tgz` here, and that was NOT IGNORED.
*.tgz
# The PyPI half of the same case, and it was still open. `dist/` above catches the artifacts
# `python -m build` writes by default; `python -m build --outdir .`, or copying an archive
# out of `dist/` to inspect its contents — which is what the wave-8 sdist work and every
# later re-measurement of that archive actually did — drops a ~220-350 KB file where nothing
# caught it. Measured with `git check-ignore -v` from a worktree: `dist/x.whl` IGNORED,
# `npm/armature-studio-0.3.0.tgz` IGNORED, and `armature_studio-0.3.0.tar.gz` at the repo
# root NOT IGNORED, nor in a subdirectory.
#
# BOTH SUFFIXES, and the second one is a correction. This block was first written with
# `*.tar.gz` alone, on the reasoning that a wheel is only ever written into `dist/` — and the
# derived census refused it in the same commit: `armature_studio-0.3.0-py3-none-any.whl` at
# the repo root and in a subdirectory both read NOT IGNORED. `pip wheel .`, `python -m build
# --outdir .` and a copied artifact all write one, and it is the file that goes to the
# registry. That is what deriving the rule is for: the reasoning was wrong and the
# measurement said so before the line shipped. `tests/test_packaging.py` derives all three
# published artifact names from `project.name` / `project.version` and `npm/package.json`
# and drives `git check-ignore` on them at the root and one level down, so a rename moves the
# rule with it rather than relying on this list being remembered.
*.tar.gz
*.whl

# npm dependency trees, at ANY depth. The rule was written `site/node_modules/` for the one
# npm tree that existed then; `npm/package.json` has carried its own `scripts.test` since
# v0.2.0 — run by ci.yml, by release.yml and by verify.ps1 — so an `npm install` there is an
# ordinary local act, and `git check-ignore` reported both `npm/node_modules/x.js` and a
# root-level `node_modules/x.js` NOT IGNORED. A bare `node_modules/` matches at every depth,
# and `tests/test_packaging.py` derives the directories npm can install into by walking for
# `package.json` rather than reading a list written here.
node_modules/

# Site (Astro/Starlight under site/). The lockfile IS committed — CI runs
# `npm ci`, so the build that runs there is the build verified on the rig.
site/dist/
site/.astro/

# Translation run cache (polyglot-mcp). The translated READMEs are committed; the
# segment cache is a local artifact and demonstrably serves stale chunks exactly
# when a number changes, so it never rides a commit.
.polyglot-cache.json

# Secrets — API keys for cloud generation never enter the repo, and neither do the two
# files a maintainer creates while debugging a publish by hand. SECURITY.md asserts that no
# long-lived registry token exists in this repository; this block is the mechanical backstop
# for that assertion, and `git check-ignore` reported `.npmrc`, `npm/.npmrc`, `.pypirc`,
# `.env.local` and `secrets.pem` all NOT IGNORED — this repo publishes to BOTH registries,
# so `.npmrc` and `.pypirc` are exactly the likely carriers. `.env` matched the exact name
# only, so every `.env.<anything>` walked past it.
.env
.env.*
.npmrc
.pypirc
# The third file both clients read. npm follows the netrc convention for registry auth and
# pip/twine reach it through requests' trust_env, so `.netrc` carries exactly the same
# long-lived token `.npmrc` and `.pypirc` do — and it was not on this list.
# `tests/test_packaging.py` derives the carriers from the registries the workflows actually
# publish to, so a third registry brings its credential file with it.
#
# BOTH SPELLINGS, because the file has two and this repository is developed on the platform
# that uses the second one. The client the comment above names decides it: measured on the
# repo venv 2026-09-04, `requests.utils.NETRC_FILES == ('.netrc', '_netrc')` — requests tries
# BOTH names, and `_netrc` is the Windows spelling (CLAUDE.md's environment block: this rig is
# the only platform this repository is developed on, and every path in it is a C:/E: path).
# Measured before this line existed, `git check-ignore -v` from a worktree: `.netrc` ->
# `.gitignore:86` IGNORED, `_netrc` -> NOT IGNORED, `npm/_netrc` -> NOT IGNORED. A one-spelling
# list is a backstop that is green on the spelling a maintainer here would actually create.
.netrc
_netrc
*.key
*.pem
*.p12
*.pfx
credentials.json

# OS / editor
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.vscode/
.idea/
.swarm/
